In Java, the Queue interface provides several methods to work with elements in a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) order. The commonly used methods include:

✅ offer(E e)
Adds an element to the end of the queue.

Returns true if the element was added successfully, or false if the queue is full (in bounded queues).

java
Copy
Edit
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer("A"); // returns true
queue.offer("B");


✅ poll()
Retrieves and removes the head of the queue.

Returns null if the queue is empty (safe alternative to remove()).

java
Copy
Edit
String item = queue.poll(); // returns "A" and removes it


✅ peek()
Retrieves but does not remove the head of the queue.

Returns null if the queue is empty.

java
Copy
Edit
String head = queue.peek(); // returns "B" but keeps it in the queue


📌 Comparison Table
| Method  | Description                       | Removes Element | Returns `null` on empty?   |
| ------- | --------------------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| `offer` | Inserts element                   | No              | ❌ (returns false if fails) |
| `poll`  | Retrieves and removes head        | ✅               | ✅                          |
| `peek`  | Retrieves but doesn't remove head | ❌               | ✅                          |


💡 Example:
java
Copy
Edit
import java.util.*;

public class QueueExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        queue.offer("One");
        queue.offer("Two");
        queue.offer("Three");

        System.out.println("Peek: " + queue.peek());  // One
        System.out.println("Poll: " + queue.poll());  // One
        System.out.println("Poll: " + queue.poll());  // Two
        System.out.println("Peek: " + queue.peek());  // Three
    }
}
